  "summary": "A distinctive tower-like minaret built in 1127 in Bukhara, serving as a prototype for later Central Asian minarets with its massive cylindrical form and decorative brickwork.",
  "description": "Standing 47 meters tall with a diameter of 9 meters at the base, the Kalyan Minaret features a tapering cylindrical shaft decorated with bands of geometric brickwork and epigraphic inscriptions. It was designed to be visible from great distances and has become an enduring symbol of Bukhara, surviving numerous earthquakes and political changes.",
  "culturalContext": "The Kalyan Minaret (Bukhara, 1127, \"Death Tower\" for its judicial throws) founded Central Asia's minaret typology: a 46m tapering brick shaft on octagonal base, ornamental bands of glazed brick (narrow-gauge formulas), and top muqarnas cornice that survived Genghis Khan's demolition of the rest of Bukhara. Prototypes: Vabkent and the Timurid towers; its silhouette is Uzbekistan's most reproduced architectural image, and UNESCO-listed Bukhara organizes tourism around it.",
